"""Terminal UI that assembles and starts an `ansible-navigator run` of an LFOps playbook.

Steps: playbook > tags > inventories > limit > confirm. The tool finds everything on its
own:

* Playbooks: `playbooks/*.yml` of the LFOps checkout that ansible-navigator mounts into
  the execution environment (`$LFOPS_DIR`, else `$LF_GITHUB/lfops`) that have hosts in
  at least one inventory.
* Tags: the roles a playbook runs (including imported playbooks, `import_role`,
  `include_role` and role dependencies), and for each role the tags documented in the
  "Tags" section of its README.
* Inventories: the ones of `$LF_GITLAB/*/ans/hosts.yml` (or of the glob in
  `$LFOPS_INVENTORIES`) with hosts in the groups the playbook targets (`hosts:` of its
  plays). An inventory without such hosts is offered if another playbook runs the role
  there, with the role as tag. Selecting it switches to that playbook, for example
  from `monitoring_plugins` to `setup_basic --tags=monitoring_plugins`.
* Limit: the hosts of the selected inventories in the groups the playbook targets,
  plus the groups these hosts are in. A typed glob such as `060-t-*` can be taken over
  as a pattern.

Playbooks, inventories, tags and limits used last are listed first. The history is kept
in `$XDG_STATE_HOME/lfops/history.json`.

Typing filters the list: entries containing the text come first, then the ones
containing its letters in order (`setbas` finds `setup_basic`), then the ones with the
text in their description.

Keys: type to filter, Up/Down/PgUp/PgDn to move, Space or Tab to (de)select, Ctrl-A to
(de)select all visible entries, Enter to continue with the selected entries or, without
a selection, with the highlighted one, Esc to go back, Ctrl-C to quit. In the playbook
step, which takes a single entry, Space and Tab continue like Enter.

On the confirm page, `c` and `d` switch `--check` and `--diff` on and off, and `f` lists
further options and takes typed text as an argument for ansible-navigator, for example
`--extra-vars=lfops__reboot_now=true`. Any further argument of the tool itself is passed
on as well, for example `lfops --check --diff`.

To get the command onto the Bash prompt instead of running it right away, and into the
shell history, load the `lfops` shell function in `~/.bashrc`:

    eval "$(command lfops --shell-init)"

The function puts the assembled command on an editable line: Enter runs it, Ctrl-C
drops it. Either way, it replaces `lfops` in the history.
"""

